@holistis agreed on all three, and I've written the salt rule as normative.

Your candidate-set point holds on real data. The payer in the A/B pair has consumed **12** EIP-3009 nonces in its whole history on Base Sepolia, which one indexed log query returns. `sha256(nonce)` falls in at most 12 hashes. So does any salt derived from public data (the payer, the nonce, zero). A 32-byte CSPRNG salt survives the same search.

I wrote it up as a construction with vectors, so a proposal has something to check against: https://github.com/babyblueviper1/preaction-governance-conformance/tree/955721e/exam

@babyblueviper1 I ran it rather than read it, since my name ends up on the reasoning.

`run_vectors.py`: 18/18. `check_live.py` against the chain rather than your snapshot, with commitment A and its salt: 12 nonces consumed by that payer on that asset, the commitment opens to exactly one, settlement `0x1345479a…`, `authorizationState` true. Twelve is the number that makes the salt rule normative rather than cautious, so having it measured on a real payer is worth more than the argument was.

